 "evidence": "Statistics of the GCM's output: total precipitation in 3-day and 6.5-hour means over the land of a T21 grid, weighted by land area. The targets' 3-day statistics interpolate two runs of different physics by the ground's temperature, as the climate targets do; their 6.5-hour statistics carry the design case's short weather to them by quantile ratios. No target was run, and no rain gauge stands behind any figure.",
 "reading_rule": "Rates are mm/h of rain at the ground, means over a T21 cell and an output window. A mean is wet at wet_threshold_mm_h or more; wet.share is the share of the land's time in wet means and wet.p10-p99 and top are the wet means' quantiles and largest, each weighted by land area. runs gives the two runs' 3-day means over years 10-19; short gives the design case's 6.5-hour years, the 3-day means their elevens make and the ratio of the first to the second at each statistic; targets gives each target's 3-day statistics, interpolated with the climate targets' interpolated_share_of_the_way, and its 6.5-hour statistics, those times the design case's ratios.",
 "assumptions": [
  "The land is every cell's land fraction, weighted by the cell's area, as the climate targets weigh the land's rain; the T21 grid cannot resolve the coasts.",
  "Each target between the two runs takes their statistics linear in the ground's temperature, as the climate targets take their climate; the hot world is the test run itself.",
  "The short weather is the design case's, near 295 K, over two years: the warmer targets take its ratios of 6.5-hour to 3-day rain.",
  "A cell's mean rain spreads a storm over 170 km and over the window: a point under a shower sees heavier, briefer rain than any of these means."
